    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "base word (verb or adjective)"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ suffix · -tion, -ment, -ness, -er / -or, -ity",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "noun · sometimes the spelling changes",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>-tion / -sion</strong> makes a noun from a verb: the action or the result. <em>educate → education, invite → invitation, inform → information, decide → decision, discuss → discussion.</em> The spelling often changes: <em>-ate → -ation</em>, <em>decide → decision</em>. The ending is said /ʃn/ (<em>-tion</em>) or /ʒn/ (<em>decision</em>).",
        "tag": "-tion · -sion"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>-ment</strong> also comes from a verb. The spelling usually stays the same: <em>move → movement, agree → agreement, pay → payment, improve → improvement, enjoy → enjoyment.</em> One exception: <em>argue → argument</em> (the <em>e</em> goes).",
        "tag": "-ment"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>-ness</strong> comes from an adjective. It names a quality or a feeling: <em>kind → kindness, ill → illness, dark → darkness, sad → sadness.</em> After a consonant + <em>y</em>, the <em>y</em> becomes <em>i</em>: <em>happy → happiness.</em>",
        "tag": "-ness"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>-er / -or</strong> comes from a verb. It names a person (or a machine) that does the action: <em>teach → teacher, work → worker, drive → driver, print → printer</em>. Some words end in <strong>-or</strong>: <em>act → actor, visit → visitor, invent → inventor, direct → director.</em> If the verb ends in <em>e</em>, add only <em>r</em>: <em>drive → driver.</em> You have to learn which words end in -or.",
        "tag": "-er · -or"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>-ity</strong> comes from an adjective, often a longer word: <em>active → activity, popular → popularity, possible → possibility, real → reality, able → ability.</em> These nouns often have no plural, but not always: <em>activities, possibilities.</em>",
        "tag": "-ity"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I made a decide.</em> → ✓ <em>I made a decision.</em> · ✗ <em>happyness</em> → ✓ <em>happiness</em> · ✗ <em>He is a good teach.</em> → ✓ <em>He is a good teacher.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "A suffix changes the word class (verb → noun) and often the stress and the sound: <em>decide</em> /dɪˈsaɪd/, <em>decision</em> /dɪˈsɪʒn/. Nouns in <em>-ness</em> and <em>-ity</em> are often uncountable (<em>kindness, popularity</em>), but a noun like <em>possibility</em> has a plural (<em>two possibilities</em>). Not every word that ends in -er is a doer: <em>number, water</em> and <em>sister</em> are just words. Verbs can also be nouns with <em>-ing</em>: <em>Swimming is fun.</em> See <a href=\"/gerunds\">-ing forms as nouns</a>. For <em>make a decision</em> and other fixed phrases, see <a href=\"/make-or-do\">Make or do</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
